{0 JavaScript interop}

Use [of_js] and [to_js] to cross the JavaScript boundary. For example,
{!Workero.Request}, {!Workero.Response}, {!Workero.Stream}, {!Workero.Env},
{!Workero.Fetcher}, {!Workero.Queue}, {!Workero.Durable_object.State} and
{!Workero.Durable_object.Sql}
wrap raw JavaScript values. Check each linked module's signature for the
conversion types. {!Workero.Promise.of_js} needs a decoder; {!Workero.URL}
parses strings and has no [of_js]/[to_js] pair.

{!Workero.js_value} is the type of a raw, unchecked JavaScript value. It is the
same type as [Js_of_ocaml.Js.Unsafe.any]. Module signatures show the jsoo name.
Use [Js_of_ocaml.Js] to read fields, call methods and convert strings.
Workero already wraps timers, [Date], random-value functions, and [console]. See
{{!page-globals}Timers, time, and randomness} and {{!page-logging}Logging}.

{@ocaml[
open Js_of_ocaml

let global name = Js.Unsafe.get Js.Unsafe.global (Js.string name)

let handler req _env _ctx =
  let raw : Workero.js_value = Workero.Request.to_js req in
  let method_ : Js.js_string Js.t = Js.Unsafe.get raw (Js.string "method") in
  let done_ : string Workero.Promise.t =
    Workero.Promise.of_js ~decode:Workero.Decode.string
      (Js.Unsafe.meth_call (global "Promise") "resolve"
         [| Js.Unsafe.inject (Js.string "done") |])
  in
  let word = Workero.Promise.await done_ in
  Workero.Response.text (Js.to_string method_ ^ " " ^ word ^ "\n")

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

{!Workero.Promise.of_js} takes a [~decode] function. It turns the resolved
JavaScript value into an OCaml value. If [decode] raises, the Promise rejects
and {!Workero.Promise.await} re-raises that exception, for example
[Invalid_argument "Workero.Decode: expected string, got number"].

{1 Decode}

{!Workero.Decode} has decoders for raw values: [js] (undecoded), [unit],
[string], [int], [float], [bool], [bytes], [option], [list] and
[field name decoder]. A decoder is a function from {!Workero.js_value}. It raises
[Invalid_argument] with the expected and the actual JavaScript type when the
value does not fit. [int] also rejects a number with a fraction or outside the
range of [int]. [bytes] rejects array elements outside 0..255. [field] reads
one property of an object or a function:

{@ocaml[
open Js_of_ocaml

let global name = Js.Unsafe.get Js.Unsafe.global (Js.string name)

let user =
  let raw =
    Js.Unsafe.obj
      [|
        ("name", Js.Unsafe.inject (Js.string "ada"));
        ("age", Js.Unsafe.inject 36);
      |]
  in
  Workero.Promise.of_js
    ~decode:(fun v -> Workero.Decode.(field "name" string v, field "age" int v))
    (Js.Unsafe.meth_call (global "Promise") "resolve" [| raw |])

let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let name, age = Workero.Promise.await user in
  Workero.Response.text (Printf.sprintf "%s is %d\n" name age)

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Add [js_of_ocaml] to [libraries] next to [workero].

{1 JSON values and environment bindings}

{!Workero.JSON.of_js} converts a JavaScript value to [Jsonkit.t] using
[JSON.stringify]. {!Workero.JSON.to_js} converts a [Jsonkit.t] back using
[JSON.parse]. These conversions copy JSON data; they do not keep the original
object. [of_js] rejects values that cannot be serialized, such as a cyclic
object or [BigInt]. [to_js] rejects non-JSON floats such as [nan].

{!Workero.Env.to_js} exposes the raw binding object if you need an API that
workero does not wrap. For ordinary bindings use the typed [of_env] functions.
{!Workero.Env.var} returns a string or [None], {!Workero.Env.secret} requires
a string and raises [Invalid_argument] if it is absent or has another type,
and {!Workero.Env.json} returns an object or array as [Jsonkit.t] (or [None]
for a non-object or missing value). See {{!page-env}Variables and secrets}.

{1 Durable Object sockets and SQL}

{!Workero.Websocket.serialize_attachment} converts a [Jsonkit.t] to a raw
JavaScript value and attaches it to a socket accepted through
{!Workero.Durable_object.State.accept_websocket}. The attachment survives
hibernation. {!Workero.Websocket.deserialize_attachment} converts it back to
[Jsonkit.t option]. [serialize_attachment] raises {!Workero.Js_error} on a
socket accepted with {!Workero.Websocket.accept}; that socket cannot hibernate.
See
{{!page-durable_objects}Durable Objects} for the socket lifecycle.

{!Workero.Durable_object.Storage.sql} provides synchronous SQLite access on a
Durable Object. {!Workero.Sql_value.to_js} and {!Workero.Sql_value.of_js}
convert individual SQL values to and from raw JavaScript values. They are not
JSON conversions: binary values use [Uint8Array], and JavaScript numbers can
lose integer precision. Check the linked signatures before passing raw values
to an external SQL API.
